          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %strong Noncommutative algebraic geometry
            is the study of ‘spaces’ represented or defined in terms of algebras, or categories. Commutative
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
            %em commutative
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasicoherent+sheaf"} quasicoherent modules
            on noncommutative locally affine space, and by affine one can think of many algebraic models, e.g.
            = succeed "-algebras;" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_\infty
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+category"} triangulated
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-category"} dg-categories
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-infinity-category"} A-infinity-categories
          %p
            Noncommutative algebraic geometry extends the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometric
            methods (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+ring"}> local ring
            s, intersection theory etc.) to study noncommutative algebras, and conversely, uses noncommutative algebras in the study of commutative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+variety"} algebraic varieties
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brauer+group"}> Brauer group
            s, noncommutative desingularizations,
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack"} stack
          %h2#relation_to_other_kinds_of_geometry Relation to other kinds of geometry
          %h3#RelationToOrdinaryAlgebraicGeometry Relation to ordinary algebraic geometry
          %p
            The direct “naive” generalization of Grothendieck-style
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
            via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaves"} sheaves
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Zariski+site"}> Zariski site
            ,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/etale+site"} etale site
            etc.) of
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+rings"} commutative rings
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} op
            to non-commutative rings does not work, for reasons discussed in some detail in (
            %a{:href => "#Reyes12"}> Reyes 12
            ). This is the reason why non-commutative algebraic geometry is phrased in other terms, mostly in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+categories"} monoidal categories
            “of (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasicoherent+sheaf"}> quasicoherent
            )
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaves"} abelian sheaves
            = succeed "\u201D)." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-rings"} 2-rings
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-algebraic+geometry"} 2-algebraic geometry
            for more.
          %h3#relation_to_noncommutative_geometry_a_la_connes Relation to noncommutative geometry a la Connes
          %p
            Noncommutative algebraic geometry may be considered a subfield of general
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+geometry"} noncommutative geometry
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alain+Connes"} Alain Connes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator+algebras"} operator algebras
            viewed as algebras of continuous, smooth or measurable functions or more general continuous global sections. There it is customary and sufficient to describe a space globally by a single algebra: smooth and continuous context allow for partition of unity so there is no need for sheaves and gluing local descriptions, to define the very spaces.
          %h2#early_history Early history
          %p
            The subject is older than mainstream
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+geometry"} noncommutative geometry
            and it is very much implicit in
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pierre+Gabriel"} Pierre Gabriel
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Des+Cat%C3%A9gories+Ab%C3%A9liennes"} Des Catégories Abéliennes
            (Bull. Soc. Math. France 1962). The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gabriel+spectrum"} Gabriel spectrum
            (consisting of isomorphism classes of injective indecomposables) amounts to a reconstruction of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noetherian+scheme"} noetherian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasiseparated+scheme"} quasiseparated
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasicompact+scheme"} quasicompact
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scheme"} scheme
            from its category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasicoherent+sheaf"} quasicoherent
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒪
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module"} module
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gabriel-Rosenberg+theorem"} Gabriel-Rosenberg theorem
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization"} localization
            functors, and corresponding subcategories (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+subcategory"} reflective
            and coreflective), and proved some theorems on localization of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasicoherent+sheaf"} quasicoherent sheaves
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Grothendieck"} Grothendieck
            taught us that
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                to study a space it is enough to have a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sheaves"} category of sheaves
                – a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} Grothendieck topos
                – on this would-be space
          %p
            Together with two Auslander-Goldman papers “
            %em Maximal orders
            ” and “
            %em The Brauer group of a commutative ring
            ” – both in Trans. AMS 1960 – Gabriel’s paper is a precursor of a more articulated development starting with Mike Artin’s “
            %em On Azumaya algebras and finte dimensional representations of rings
            ” (J. Alg. 1969). Artin’s 1969 paper characterizes
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Azumaya+algebra"} Azumaya algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
            to
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation"} representation
            %em Rings with polynomial identities
            ” (Marcel Dekker 1973) and his paper “
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %em
                The invariant theory of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ×
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\times n
                matrices
            %em A version of Zariski’s main theorem for PI rings
            ” (Amer. J. Math. 1979). The link with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+theory"} representation theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/George+Bergman"} George Bergman
            %em The simple Artinian spectrum of a finite dimensional algebra
            ” (in Dekker Lect. Notes 51, 1979) and culminated in Aidan Schofield’s book “
            %em Representations of rings over skew fields
            ” (1985). These developments have been influential for recent work in ‘
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+scheme"}> formal
            ’ noncommutative geometry, such as
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"} Maxim Kontsevich
            %em
              Formal (non)commutative symplectic geometry“ (1993),
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joachim+Cuntz"} Joachim Cuntz
              and
              = succeed "\u2018s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Quillen"} Daniel Quillen
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-free+algebra"} quasi-free algebra
          %p
            In the 70ties,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization"} localization
            theory was popular among ring theorists, and, several people tried to generalize
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure+sheaf"} structure sheaves
            to noncommutative algebras. Gabriel-like localizations via alternative formalism of “kernel functors” (Goldman 1969) led to the books by Jonathan Golan “
            %em Localization of noncommutative rings
            ” (Marcel Dekker 1975) and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fred+Van+Oystaeyen"} Fred Van Oystaeyen
            “
            %em Prime spectra in noncommutative algebra
            ” (Springer LNM 444, 1975), and making the link with the Artin-Procesi approach, the book
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fred+Van+Oystaeyen"}> Fred Van Oystaeyen
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alain+Verschoren"} Alain Verschoren
              = succeed "," do
                %em Non-commutative algebraic geometry
          %p
            Localizations were viewed as analogues of open sets. However, the lattice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsion+theories"} torsion theories
            is not
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributive+lattice"} distributive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent"} descent
            theorem was proved by van Oystaeyen and Willaert and independently in a work of A. Rosenberg in Stockholm (1988). It is sort of generalized
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaf
            condition for quasicoherent modules, with respect to
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cover"} cover
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonad"} comonad
            induced by a conservative family of flat localizationshaving right adjoint, which in the Gabriel localization clearly satisfy the conditions for Beck’s
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonadicity+theorem"} comonadicity theorem
          %p
            Cohn’s universal localizations were used by Paul Cohn in “
            %em The affine scheme of a general ring
            ” (in Springer LNM 753, 1979) and later by Claus Ringel and Aidan Schofield as mentioned before. In England, Goldie’s localization theory led to the theory of ‘cliques’ and ‘clans’ of prime ideals (Jategaonkar, Hajarnavis and others) which may in retrospect be viewed as influential to present local description via
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver"} quiver
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-infinity-category"} A-infinity structures
          %p
            Around 1980, the Leningrad school of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integrable+system"} integrable system
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+groups"} quantum groups
            %strong affine noncommutative geometry
            (spaces represented by a single nongraded ring), moreover the actions of group-like objects are considered starting the subject of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+noncommutative+algebraic+geometry"} equivariant noncommutative algebraic geometry
          %p
            In the early 80ties maximal orders were revisited as noncommutative versions of normal
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variety"} varieties
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ken+A.+Brown"} Ken A. Brown
            and others), Artin-Schelter regular algebras and, finally, Auslander-regular rings.
          %p
            In this vein, around 1989, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+projective+geometry"} noncommutative projective geometry
            was developed by Artin, Zhang and collaborators, where Serre’s theorem on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Proj
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Proj
            of a graded ring is taken as a definition in the noncommutative case:
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasicoherent+sheaf"} quasicoherent sheaves
            over a
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Proj
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Proj
            of a graded ring is the category of graded modules modulo the modules of finite length. They assume strong regularity properties on graded rings, limiting to the geometry which is very close to commutative projective geometry. Using
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ore+localization"} Ore localization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F.+van+Oystaeyen"} F. van Oystaeyen
            has defined another version of noncommutative projective geometry, based on his notion of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/schematic+algebra"} schematic algebra
          %p
            A huge project started, trying to classify Auslander-regular algebras, graded-connected and generated in degree one (that is, noncommutative analogons of projective n-space) for small dimensions n. In dimension 3, the initial work was done by Artin and Schelter, culminating in the influential papers (ATV1), (ATV2) by Mike Artin, John Tate and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michel+Van+den+Bergh"} Michel Van den Bergh
            (see bibl.). In this classification a class of algebras re-appeared which were initially discovered by Odeskii and Feigin, the so called Sklyanin algebras. Ring-theoretic investigations of the Sklyanin algebras were done by Tate and Van den Bergh (Noetherianity for all dimensions n) and in dimension 4 a geometric study via ‘point’ and ‘line’ modules was carried out by Thierry Levasseur,
            %span.newWikiWord
              Toby Stafford
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Toby+Stafford"} ?
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Smith"} Paul Smith
          %h2#approaches Approaches
          %h3#spectrum_of_an_abelian_category Spectrum of an abelian category
          %p
            When one is serious about the point of view that every space is represented by some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
            and that every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
            represents a (noncommutative) space this way, one wants to find the
            %em spectrum
            of an abelian category in the way that one forms the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum+%28geometry%29"} spectrum (geometry)
            of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
          %p
            Motivated by this,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A.+L.+Rosenberg"} A. L. Rosenberg
            has developed many spectral constructions extending from the left spectrum of a ring to various spectra of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+category"} triangulated categories
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+category"} right exact categories
            (in his sense) and created a categorical version of a notion of (quasicompact relative)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+scheme"} noncommutative scheme
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            over a base category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            (viewed as category of sheaves on
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Spec
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec k
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+cookbook"} spectral cookbook
          %h3#sheaves_of_sets_on_a_category
            Sheaves of sets on a
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
          %p
            A space in the sense of a Grothendieck school is a sheaf
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            of sets on the category of affine schemes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Aff
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Aff
            equipped with some subcanonical Grothendieck topology and which is locally affine, i.e. there is a cover of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            (in the sense of induced Grothendieck topology on the category of presheaves) by representables. The noncommutative analogue of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Aff
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Aff
            is the opposite to the category of noncommutative unital rings
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi NAff
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi Ring
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} NAff = Ring^{op}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi NAff
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} NAff
            are not making classes which satisfy the axioms for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topology"} Grothendieck topology
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            can be equipped with a structure of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Q-category"} Q-category
          %h3#DerivedNonCommutativeGeometry Derived noncommutative geometry
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+noncommutative+geometry"} Derived noncommutative geometry
            is a subject related to the commutative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+algebraic+geometry"} derived algebraic geometry
            of
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Carlos+Simpson"} C. Simpson
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hochschild+homology"} Hochschild homology
            play a major role.
          %p
            Here one thinks of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-categories"} dg-categories
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-infinity+categories"} A-infinity categories
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spectra"} Spectra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+categories"} enriched categories
            and ultimately of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} stable (∞,1)-categories
            as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+duals"} formal duals
            to non-commutative derived spaces:
          %p
            Any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+presentable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} locally presentable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} stable (∞,1)-category
            may be regarded as a stable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            (the “stable Giraud theorem”, see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "stable+infinity-category#StabGiraud"} there
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+dual"} formal dual
            to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+category"} triangulated category
            or rather to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} stable (∞,1)-category
            is directly analogous to the way
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
            and in particular
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+topos+theory"} higher topos theory
            characterizes generalized spaces as formal duals of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/toposes"} toposes
          %p
            More in detail, every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} stable (∞,1)-category
            with a set of generators is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+%28infinity%2C1%29-categories"} equivalently
            the
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-modules"} (∞,1)-modules
            over an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-%E2%88%9E+algebra"} A-∞ algebra
            (or rather
            = succeed "-algebroid," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_\infty
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "stable+infinity-category#SchwedeShipley"} Schwede-Shipley 01
            %a{:href => "stable+infinity-category#AsCategoriesOfModules"} there
            for more).
          %p
            The notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+motive"} noncommutative motive
            as localizations of stable
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "noncommutative+motive#AsUniversalAditiveInvariant"} there
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "noncommutative+motive#BlumbergGepnerTabuada10"} Blumberg-Gepner-Tabuada 10
          %p
            The most nontrivial result seem to be a noncommutative version of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/degeneration+of+Hodge-to-de-Rham+spectral+sequence"} degeneration of Hodge-to-de-Rham spectral sequence
            %a{:href => "http://front.math.ucdavis.edu/0611.5623"} proved
            by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D.+Kaledin"} D. Kaledin
          %h4#homological_mirror_symmetry Homological mirror symmetry
          %p
            %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Homological_mirror_symmetry"} Homological mirror symmetry
            by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"} Maxim Kontsevich
            is a source of many
            %em examples
            of spaces of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+algebraic+geometry"} derived algebraic geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+category"} triangulated categories
            or their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-category"} dg-
            or
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_\infty
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enhanced+triangulated+category"} enhancements
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+category"} derived categories
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+sheaf"} coherent sheaves
            and their deformations like
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Landau-Ginzburg+model"} Landau-Ginzburg model
            = succeed "(1977/8)," do
              %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexander_Beilinson"} Beilinson
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mikhail+Kapranov"} Mikhail Kapranov
            (1985-),
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dmitri+Orlov"} Dmitri Orlov
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexei+Bondal"} Alexei Bondal
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michel+van+den+Bergh"} Michel van den Bergh
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Polishchuk"} Alexander Polishchuk
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tom+Bridgeland"} Tom Bridgeland
            and others.
          %h3#other_motivations Other motivations
          %p
            Another big source of examples is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"} deformation quantization
            and related topics involving usage of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_\infty
            and
            = succeed "-algebras." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
            %span.newWikiWord
              Lieven le Bruyn
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Lieven+le+Bruyn"} ?
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                Arfinn Laudal
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Arfinn+Laudal"} ?
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %div
            %p
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+between+algebra+and+geometry"} duality between
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \;
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
            %table{:style => "margin:auto"}
              %thead
                %tr
                  %th
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mphantom
                            %mi A
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phantom{A}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mphantom
                            %mi A
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phantom{A}
                  %th
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mphantom
                            %mi A
